  Representative solar radio event types are classified by their typical flux
  density at \SI{610}{\mega\hertz} and compared against the simulation's
  minimum detectable flux ($\Delta S_{\rm min}\approx0.058$~SFU for 50 dumps).
  All event categories, including the quiet Sun at $\approx100$~SFU, are
  detectable at extremely high significance in a single integration dump.
  Type~III and Type~II bursts exceed the noise floor by factors of
  $10^4$--$10^5$.}
  \label{tab:detectability}
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.3}
  \begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{l c c >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Event type} & \textbf{Typical flux (SFU)} & \textbf{SNR (1 dump)} & \textbf{Detectability} \\
    \midrule
    Quiet Sun               & $\sim$100           & $\sim$1\,700  & Always detected \\
    Low-level active region & $\sim$300           & $\sim$5\,200  & Easily detected \\
    Type~III (moderate)     & $\sim$1\,000        & $\sim$17\,200 & Strongly detected \\
    Type~III (intense)      & $\sim$10\,000       & $\sim$172\,000& Scale-saturating \\
    Type~II (shock)         & 500-5\,000         & 8\,600-86\,000 & Time-extended \\
    Type~IV (continuum)     & $\sim$200-1\,000   & 3\,400-17\,200 & Detected with high confidence \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabularx}
\end{table}

All event categories in Table~\ref{tab:detectability} are detectable at extremely
high significance in a single integration dump. The quiet Sun itself-the weakest
source in the table-registers at SNR $\approx1\,700$ per dump. This confirms
that the Learmonth \SI{610}{\mega\hertz} monitoring channel, which the simulation
faithfully replicates, is designed to detect quiet-Sun flux modulations with
high fidelity, and that any Type~II or Type~III burst occurring during the transit
would be unmistakable.

\subsection{Dynamic Range of the Dirty Image}

The dynamic range (DR) of a dirty image is the ratio of the peak brightness to the
RMS of the off-source background:
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{DR} = \frac{I_{\rm peak}}{\sigma_{\rm bg}}.
  \label{eq:dynamic-range}
\end{equation}
For the compact east-west array (Case~1), the dominant image artefact is not
thermal noise but the north-south elongation of the dirty beam: the PSF peak
sidelobe is a significant fraction of the main-lobe amplitude, and it appears
as a systematic stripe rather than as random noise. Consequently, the image DR
is limited by the PSF sidelobe level rather than by receiver noise.
Additional integration time does not help in this regime; only more baselines
(better UV coverage) or deconvolution (\textsc{clean}) can improve it.

Transitioning to the graded-cross layout (baselines to \SI{130}{\metre},
Section~\ref{sec:sweep}) increases the image DR by approximately
$20~\si{dB}$ because the more uniform 2D UV coverage suppresses PSF sidelobes
and eliminates the elongation artefact. This is the quantitative motivation for the
array upgrade discussed in the Conclusion.

\subsection{Interferometric Advantage for Burst Localisation}

A total-power (single-dish) radiometer can detect a burst but cannot localise it
on the solar disk. The compact interferometer adds a spatial dimension: the
fringe amplitude on the \SI{24}{\metre} baseline is sensitive to structures larger than
$\lambda/b\approx1.2^\circ$, larger than the solar diameter ($0.53^\circ$), meaning the
compact array detects but cannot resolve solar disk structure.
Although the compact east-west array cannot image the full disk, it can
distinguish between a burst originating at the disk centre (maximum fringe
amplitude) and one at the limb (reduced amplitude due to partial resolution). The
extended graded cross, reaching \SI{130}{\metre}, reduces the resolution element
to $\lambda/b_{\rm max}\approx0.22^\circ$, sufficient to localise a burst to a
fraction of the solar radius. Table~\ref{tab:detectability} and the dynamic-range
analysis together define the sensitivity envelope within which such localisation is
possible.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Operational Simplicity and Real-Time Data Accessibility}
\label{sec:workflow}

\subsection{From Archive to Dirty Image in a Single Notebook}

A central design goal of the pipeline is that a user unfamiliar with aperture
synthesis should be able to reproduce every result in this thesis by editing a
single cell and running the notebook from top to bottom. This contrasts with the
typical workflow of professional radio interferometry packages such as CASA or
AIPS, which require dozens of calibration steps, separate pipeline scripts, and
significant domain expertise.

The complete workflow for a new observation date is as follows.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item \textbf{Download the SWS daily file.} The Learmonth Solar Radio Observatory
        archives raw spectral patrol files at the Space Weather Services (SWS) FTP
        server, one ASCII file per station per day in a well-documented format with
        \texttt{//////} tokens marking data gaps. No registration is required.
  \item \textbf{Update Cell~0.} Change \texttt{NGAY} (the date string, format
        \texttt{DD-MM-YYYY}) and \texttt{DEC\_SUN\_DEG} (the solar declination for
        that date, available from any online ephemeris or computed as
        $\delta\approx-23.45^\circ\cos(360^\circ(d+10)/365.25)$ where $d$ is the
        Julian day). All downstream cells inherit these two values without any other
        modification.
  \item \textbf{Run all cells.} The notebook reads and preprocesses the SWS file
        (gap-flag removal, cubic spline interpolation), synthesises per-antenna
        voltages and noise, correlates to form visibilities, computes UV coverage
        and the dirty image, and exports all figures and numerical data in a single
        top-to-bottom execution. Total runtime on a mid-range laptop is
        under two minutes for \texttt{N\_SAMPLES}$=50\,000$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Configuration Centralisation versus \textsc{APSYNSIM} Setup Complexity}

Setting up \textsc{APSYNSIM} for a new array geometry requires the user to: (a)
manually position antenna elements by clicking the GUI canvas, (b) choose a source
model interactively, (c) read off beam and image quality visually from the screen,
and (d) repeat the entire session for each configuration. This interactive paradigm
is pedagogically valuable but not reproducible in the scientific sense: two users
may position antennas slightly differently, apply different amounts of
\textsc{clean}, or use different zoom levels, producing results that cannot be
compared directly.

In the present pipeline every parameter is explicitly declared in Cell~0 and
version-controlled with the \texttt{.ipynb} file. Table~\ref{tab:workflow-compare}
compares the two workflows.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption[Workflow comparison: \textsc{APSYNSIM} versus this work's pipeline]{Workflow comparison: \textsc{APSYNSIM} versus this work's pipeline.\\[4pt]
  A typical instrument-design study covering five array geometries on two
  observation dates. Steps include specifying the array, loading source data,
  setting observation parameters, running the simulation, exporting results,
  reproducing results later, and changing the observation date.}
  \label{tab:workflow-compare}
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.3}
  \begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{l X X}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Step} & \textbf{\textsc{APSYNSIM}} & \textbf{This Work} \\
    \midrule
    Specify array
      & Click antenna positions on GUI canvas (manual, session-by-session)
      & Edit one list of ENU coordinates in Cell~0; or call a preset function \\
    Load source data
      & Select from built-in menu; adjust Gaussian parameters interactively
      & Automatic: \texttt{sws.load\_day(NGAY)} reads real flux from file \\
    Set observation parameters
      & Adjust GUI sliders for latitude, HA, declination, wavelength
      & Edit 2-3 variables in Cell~0; all cells inherit automatically \\
    Run simulation
      & Immediate (GUI redraws on each mouse event)
      & Run all cells: $<\SI{2}{\minute}$ for 50\,000 samples \\
    Export results
      & Screenshot / manual FITS save (APSYNTRUE only)
      & Automatic: \texttt{.npz} + PNG per geometry, summary \texttt{.txt} \\
    Reproduce later
      & Re-run manually; no guaranteed parameter record
      & Re-execute \texttt{.ipynb}; identical results guaranteed \\
    Change observation date
      & Full manual re-setup required
      & Edit \texttt{NGAY} and \texttt{DEC\_SUN\_DEG} in Cell~0; re-run \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\subsection{Sensitivity to Parameter Changes and Rapid Prototyping}

Because all parameters live in one cell, a parameter sensitivity study is
straightforward. Changing $T_{\rm sys}$ from $\SI{150}{\kelvin}$ to
$\SI{200}{\kelvin}$ increases the noise floor by a factor of
$\sqrt{200/150}\approx1.15$ and reduces the per-dump SNR by $\approx1.2~\si{dB}$.
Increasing \texttt{N\_SAMPLES} from 50\,000 to 200\,000 improves temporal
resolution fourfold at the cost of proportionally more memory and runtime.
Changing \texttt{LAT\_DEG} to $-26.7^\circ$ would simulate the Murchison
Radio-astronomy Observatory; the ENU$\rightarrow$XYZ rotation and the transit
window update automatically. None of these changes requires modifying more than
one line of code; none are possible in a GUI-only tool without full manual
re-interaction.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Limitations of the Current Implementation}
\label{sec:limitations}

For completeness, the principal simplifications and their expected impact on results
are listed below.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item \textbf{Single spectral channel.} The simulation models a single
        $\SI{1}{\mega\hertz}$ channel at $\SI{610}{\mega\hertz}$. Bandwidth
        smearing-which causes decorrelation at angular offsets from the phase
        centre proportional to $\Delta\nu/\nu$-is not modelled. For
        $\Delta\nu/\nu = 10^6/610\times10^6 \approx 1.6\times10^{-3}$ and
        baselines below $\SI{130}{\metre}$, the smearing at a $0.5^\circ$ offset
        is $\lesssim2\,\%$ of the fringe amplitude, which is negligible for the
        present validation study.
  \item \textbf{No ionospheric phase screen.} Differential ionospheric
        phase between antennas scales as $\Delta\phi \approx \Delta{\rm TEC}/\nu^2$,
        where $\Delta{\rm TEC}$ is the differential total electron content. For
        baselines below $\SI{130}{\metre}$ at $\SI{610}{\mega\hertz}$, the expected
        differential phase is $\lesssim1^\circ$ during undisturbed conditions, which
        is below the current precision requirement.
  \item \textbf{Independent-element assumption.} The mutual-coupling study of
        Chapter~\ref{ch:antenna} showed coupling below $-78~\si{dB}$ beyond
        $\SI{8}{\metre}$. No Jones-matrix correction is applied even at the shortest
        $\SI{8}{\metre}$ baseline, where the coupling could in principle contribute
        a $\sim10^{-4}$ fractional amplitude error-far below any other error source.
  \item \textbf{No deconvolution.} The imaging chain produces a dirty image.
        \textsc{clean} or a compressed-sensing solver would be required to recover
        the true sky brightness and to achieve noise-limited dynamic range.
  \item \textbf{Narrow-field assumption.} The DFT imaging assumes a flat sky
        (small $w$-term). The solar diameter ($0.53^\circ$) is well within the
        regime where the $w$-approximation is valid for baselines below
        $\SI{130}{\metre}$, but extension to wider fields or longer baselines would
        require $w$-projection.
  \item \textbf{Perfect clock synchronisation.}
        The voltage model of Eq.~\eqref{eq:vk} applies a common, deterministic
        time axis to all antennas (\texttt{CLOCK\_OFFSET\_NS}$=[0,0,0,0]$~ns).
        In real hardware each digitiser runs on its own oscillator; a differential
        clock drift $\varepsilon$ between any two antennas adds a time-varying
        phase to their shared visibility and can reduce the time-averaged fringe
        amplitude to zero within a single integration dump-independently of SNR.
        A quantitative sensitivity study is presented in
        Section~\ref{sec:clock-offset}.
\end{enumerate}

These six limitations define the natural extensions of the present work and are
addressed directly in the Future Work section of the Conclusion.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Comparison with Real-Setup Measurements: Digital vs.\ Analogue Interferometry}
\label{sec:real-comparison}

The preceding sections verify the simulation against \emph{analytic} predictions
(fringe period, $\jinc$ visibility, PSF identity). This section extends that comparison
to an \emph{experimental} recording from the two-element analogue interferometer
deployed at Hoa~L\d{a}c on 20~March~2025-the same date used throughout this chapter.
This comparison is a \emph{hardware sanity check}, not a full end-to-end calibrated
validation: the hardware gain chain was not independently calibrated, so only a
gain-independent metric (solar excess above noise floor) can be meaningfully compared.

\subsection{The Distinction Between Digital and Analogue Interferometry}

In \emph{analogue interferometry} the two antenna signals are combined at radio
frequency by a coaxial power combiner. The combined signal drives a broadband power
detector; the output is a slowly varying DC voltage proportional to the total received
power including the fringe modulation. The phase relationship between the two signals
is fixed by the physical cable lengths and the geometry of the array; it cannot be
adjusted post-detection. The instrument is a correlating radiometer realised entirely
in hardware.

In \emph{digital interferometry} (the approach of this thesis) the two signals are
digitised independently as complex IQ baseband voltages. The cross-correlation is
computed in software, allowing arbitrary post-processing: fringe stopping, phase
calibration, baseline selection, and imaging. The key advantage is that a single
recording can be reprocessed with different array parameters, different imaging
algorithms, or additional baselines added later, without any physical change to the
hardware.

\subsection{Average Power on 20~March~2025}

The simulation computes the beam-weighted transit-average signal power over the
observation window $|H|\leq30^\circ$ (approximately 10:00-14:00 local time,
matching the hardware recording window):
\begin{equation}
  \overline{P}_{\rm sim} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}
    S_\nu(t_n)\,A_e\,A_n\!\bigl(\theta_z(t_n)\bigr)\,\Delta\nu,
  \label{eq:pavg-sim}
\end{equation}
where $A_n(\theta_z)$ is the normalised power beam pattern from the 4NEC2 simulation.
Because the absolute power at the hardware receiver port depends on the unknown total
gain of the analogue chain (LNA, cables, downconverter), a direct watt-to-watt
comparison is not feasible without an end-to-end gain calibration.  Instead, the
comparison uses the \emph{solar excess above the thermal noise floor},
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{E} = 10\log_{10}\!\left(1 + \frac{\overline{P}_{\rm sim}}{P_{\rm noise}}\right)
  \quad[\mathrm{dB}],
  \label{eq:excess}
\end{equation}
where $P_{\rm noise} = k_B\,T_{\rm sys}\,\Delta\nu$.  This quantity is
gain-independent: any common multiplicative factor in the hardware chain cancels
between signal and noise, leaving only the ratio $\overline{P}_{\rm sim}/P_{\rm noise}$.
From the hardware trace recorded at Hoa~L\d{a}c on 20~March~2025, the corresponding
measurement is read directly as the difference between the transit envelope peak and
the pre-transit noise baseline (both in the same dB scale of the recording).

Table~\ref{tab:power-comparison} compares $\mathcal{E}$ from simulation and measurement.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption[Solar excess for 20~March~2025: simulation versus hardware measurement]{Solar excess for 20~March~2025: simulation versus hardware measurement.\\[4pt]
  The solar excess $\mathcal{E}$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:excess}) is the ratio of
  time-averaged signal power to the thermal noise floor, expressed in dB.
  Simulation: 4NEC2 beam model, archived SWS Learmonth flux,
  $T_{\rm sys}=\SI{150}{\kelvin}$, transit window $|H|\leq30^\circ$.
  Hardware: two-element Yagi analogue interferometer at Hoa~L\d{a}c on
  20~March~2025, operated without a dedicated low-noise amplifier.
  The factor-of-$23.8\times$ SNR gap implies an effective hardware system
  temperature of approximately \SI{3600}{\kelvin}.}
  \label{tab:power-comparison}
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.3}
  \begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{l c >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Source} & \textbf{Solar excess $\mathcal{E}$ (dB)} & \textbf{Notes} \\
    \midrule
    Simulation (this work)
      & $0.586$
      & Eq.~\eqref{eq:excess}; $\overline{P}_{\rm sim}/P_{\rm noise}=14.3\,\%$ \\
    Hardware (Hoa~L\d{a}c, 20 Mar 2025)
      & $0.026$
      & Peak envelope minus baseline; $P_{\rm sig}/P_{\rm noise}=0.60\,\%$ \\
    Ratio (linear SNR)
      & $23.8\times$
      & Implies $T_{\rm sys,eff}\approx\SI{3600}{\kelvin}$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\subsection{Interpretation}

Table~\ref{tab:power-comparison} reveals a factor of $23.8\times$ between the
simulated and measured solar excess.  This discrepancy is not a failure of the Friis
model; it is a direct measurement of the \emph{effective system temperature gap}
between the idealised pipeline ($T_{\rm sys}=\SI{150}{\kelvin}$) and the real
consumer-grade hardware.

The real receiver is a software-defined radio (SDR) tuner connected through passive
coaxial splitters and cables, with no dedicated low-noise amplifier (LNA).
For such a chain, the effective system temperature is
\begin{equation}
  T_{\rm sys,eff} \approx \frac{T_{\rm ant}}{L_{\rm cable}}
    + T_0\!\left(1-\frac{1}{L_{\rm cable}}\right)
    + T_{\rm rx},
  \label{eq:tsys-eff}
\end{equation}
where $T_{\rm ant}\approx\SI{150}{\kelvin}$ (sky and ground spillover),
$L_{\rm cable}$ is the cable loss factor, $T_0=\SI{290}{\kelvin}$, and
$T_{\rm rx}=(F-1)\,T_0$, where $F$ (dimensionless) is the \emph{linear} noise
factor related to the noise figure $\mathrm{NF}$ in dB by $F=10^{\mathrm{NF}/10}$.
A typical RTL-SDR at \SI{610}{\mega\hertz} has
$\mathrm{NF}\approx8$--$12~\si{\decibel}$ ($F\approx6.3$--$15.8$),
giving $T_{\rm rx}\approx1\,500$--$4\,300~\si{\kelvin}$.
With $\SI{3}{\decibel}$ of cable loss ($L_{\rm cable}=2$) and $T_{\rm rx}=\SI{2\,900}{\kelvin}$,
Eq.~\eqref{eq:tsys-eff} gives $T_{\rm sys,eff}\approx75+145+5\,800\approx\SI{6\,000}{\kelvin}$.
More conservatively, inverting the ratio in Table~\ref{tab:power-comparison},
\begin{equation}
  T_{\rm sys,eff} = T_{\rm sys,model}
    \times\frac{(\overline{P}_{\rm sim}/P_{\rm noise})_{\rm sim}}
               {(\overline{P}_{\rm sig}/P_{\rm noise})_{\rm meas}}
  = 150\times23.8 \approx \SI{3\,600}{\kelvin},
  \label{eq:tsys-backcalc}
\end{equation}
which is the effective system temperature back-calculated from the measurement.
This value is fully consistent with SDR hardware operating at UHF without an LNA
and with typical cable losses of $2$-$4~\si{\decibel}$.

The simulation is therefore physically correct: it predicts the solar signal power
accurately (confirmed independently by the fringe-period and $\jinc$-visibility
benchmarks).  The $23.8\times$ SNR gap is entirely attributable to $T_{\rm sys}$
calibration.  Replacing the consumer SDR with an LNA of noise figure
$\lesssim\SI{1}{\decibel}$ ($T_{\rm rx}\approx\SI{75}{\kelvin}$) would reduce
$T_{\rm sys,eff}$ to $\approx\SI{300}{\kelvin}$ and increase the measured solar
excess to $\approx0.026\times(3\,600/300)\approx\SI{0.31}{\decibel}$, an improvement
of $12\times$ in SNR, comparable to the simulation model.

Beyond total power, the digital pipeline recovers information inaccessible to the
analogue chain: the $\sim$14-minute fringe modulation and the $\jinc$-shaped
visibility amplitude versus baseline length.  This comparison, total power envelope
(analogue, broad bell curve) versus fringe pattern and visibility (digital), is the
clearest demonstration of the complementary value of digital interferometry for solar
burst science.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Clock-Offset Sensitivity Study}
\label{sec:clock-offset}

The voltage model of Chapter~\ref{ch:pipeline} treats all antennas as sharing a
perfect common clock (\texttt{CLOCK\_OFFSET\_NS}$=[0,0,0,0]$~ns).  This section
uses the clock-offset injection facility (Eq.~\eqref{eq:clock-phase}) to quantify
how realistic oscillator imperfections would degrade interferometric measurements,
and to place the hardware coherence requirement on a quantitative footing.

\subsection{Static Offset: Phase Rotation Without Amplitude Loss}

A static clock offset $\tau_{ij}$ between the digitisers of antennas $i$ and $j$
introduces a constant phase error on the baseline visibility
(Eq.~\eqref{eq:vis-clock}):
\begin{equation}
  \Delta\phi_{ij} = 2\pi f_c\,\tau_{ij}
  \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
  \frac{\Delta\phi_{ij}}{1~\mathrm{ns}}
    = 2\pi\times\SI{610}{\mega\hertz}\times10^{-9}~\mathrm{s}
    \approx 219.6^\circ\,\mathrm{ns}^{-1}.
  \label{eq:static-phase}
\end{equation}
Table~\ref{tab:clock-static} evaluates Eq.~\eqref{eq:static-phase} for
representative offsets.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption[Static clock offset versus visibility phase error at 610~MHz]{Static clock offset versus visibility phase error at 610~MHz.\\[4pt]
  A static differential clock offset $\tau_{ij}$ between two digitisers
  introduces a constant phase rotation $\Delta\phi_{ij}=2\pi f_c\tau_{ij}$
  on the corresponding baseline visibility; the amplitude is unaffected.
  At $f_c=\SI{610}{\mega\hertz}$, even a 0.1~ns offset produces a $22^\circ$
  phase error, sufficient to displace a simulated point source by more than
  its own diameter along the 8-m baseline direction.}
  \label{tab:clock-static}
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
  \begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{c c >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Offset $\tau_{ij}$} & \textbf{Phase error $\Delta\phi_{ij}$}
      & \textbf{Dirty-image effect} \\
    \midrule
    $0$~ns    & $0^\circ$    & No effect; phase centre correct \\
    $0.1$~ns  & $22^\circ$   & Mild apparent source position shift \\
    $0.5$~ns  & $110^\circ$  & Source displaced along baseline direction \\
    $1$~ns    & $220^\circ$  & Large position error; image artefacts \\
    $1.64$~ns & $360^\circ$  & Full phase cycle; source wraps to original position \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabularx}
\end{table}

A static phase error shifts the apparent source position along the baseline
direction by $\Delta\theta\approx\Delta\phi_{ij}/(2\pi\,b_{ij}/\lambda)$.
For the \SI{8}{\metre} baseline ($b_{ij}/\lambda\approx16.3$) and a $1$~ns
offset ($\Delta\phi_{ij}\approx3.83$~rad), this gives
$\Delta\theta\approx3.83/(2\pi\times16.3)\approx2.1^\circ$-larger than the
solar diameter ($0.53^\circ$).  A single-nanosecond timing error therefore
places the simulated Sun at the wrong sky position by nearly four solar diameters.
Static offsets can be removed by a point-source phase calibration, but only if
a suitable calibrator transit is available.

\subsection{Clock Drift: Fringe Washing and the Coherence Time}

A more fundamental degradation arises when the two oscillators run at slightly
different \emph{rates}.  Let $\varepsilon$ (dimensionless, expressed in parts
per million, ppm) be the fractional frequency offset between the digitisers of
antennas $i$ and $j$.  The differential delay then grows as
$\tau_{ij}(t)=\tau_{ij}(0)+\varepsilon\,t$, and the instantaneous visibility
phase advances continuously.  Within one integration dump of duration
$\Delta t_{\mathrm{dump}}=\SI{43.2}{\second}$, the phase changes by
$2\pi f_c\,\varepsilon\,\Delta t_{\mathrm{dump}}$ from the first sample to
the last.  Time-averaging over the dump reduces the fringe amplitude by the
coherence factor
\begin{equation}
  \eta_{\mathrm{coh}}
  = \left|\operatorname{sinc}\!\bigl(f_c\,\varepsilon\,\Delta t_{\mathrm{dump}}\bigr)\right|,
  \label{eq:coherence}
\end{equation}
where $\operatorname{sinc}(x)\equiv\sin(\pi x)/(\pi x)$.  The amplitude falls
to zero (complete fringe washing) when
$f_c\,\varepsilon\,\Delta t_{\mathrm{dump}}=1$, defining the
\emph{coherence time}
\begin{equation}
  \Delta t_{\mathrm{coh}} = \frac{1}{f_c\,\varepsilon}.
  \label{eq:tcoh}
\end{equation}

Table~\ref{tab:clock-drift} evaluates Eqs.~\eqref{eq:coherence} and
\eqref{eq:tcoh} for oscillator accuracies representative of consumer and
laboratory hardware at $f_c=\SI{610}{\mega\hertz}$,
$\Delta t_{\mathrm{dump}}=\SI{43.2}{\second}$.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption[Clock drift versus fringe coherence factor at 610~MHz (43.2-s dump)]{Clock drift versus fringe coherence factor at 610~MHz (43.2-s dump).\\[4pt]
  The fringe coherence factor $\eta_{\rm coh}$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:coherence})
  is evaluated as a function of oscillator fractional frequency offset
  $\varepsilon$ for a 43.2-second integration dump at $f_c=\SI{610}{\mega\hertz}$.
  Consumer SDR crystals (1~ppm drift) produce complete fringe washing
  ($\eta_{\rm coh}\approx0$) within a single dump; maintaining
  $\eta_{\rm coh}>0.9$ requires a GPSDO or equivalent precision oscillator
  with $\varepsilon\lesssim10$~ppt.}
  \label{tab:clock-drift}
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
  \begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{l c c c >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Drift $\varepsilon$} & \textbf{$\Delta t_{\rm coh}$}
      & \textbf{$f_c\varepsilon\Delta t_{\rm dump}$}
      & \textbf{$\eta_{\rm coh}$} & \textbf{Oscillator class} \\
    \midrule
    $1$~ppm         & $1.6$~ms         & $26\,352$  & $\approx 0$   & Consumer SDR crystal \\
    $0.1$~ppm       & $16$~ms          & $2\,635$   & $\approx 0$   & TCXO (temperature compensated) \\
    $1$~ppb         & $1.6$~s          & $26.4$     & $\approx 0$   & OCXO (oven controlled) \\
    $10$~ppt        & $164$~s          & $0.264$    & $0.88$        & Precision OCXO \\
    $1$~ppt         & $1\,640$~s       & $0.026$    & $\approx 1$   & GPSDO (GPS-disciplined) \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabularx}
\end{table}

The requirement for $\eta_{\mathrm{coh}}>0.9$ over the \SI{43.2}{\second} dump
is $\varepsilon<10$~ppt ($10^{-11}$), corresponding to a coherence time
$\Delta t_{\mathrm{coh}}>164$~s.  This level of stability is achievable with a
GPS-disciplined oscillator (GPSDO) or a precision oven-controlled crystal
(OCXO) with external $10$~MHz reference injection.  Consumer oscillators-including
the crystal in a typical RTL-SDR, rated at approximately $0.5$-$1$~ppm-produce
$\Delta t_{\mathrm{coh}}\lesssim\SI{3}{\milli\second}$, more than four orders
of magnitude shorter than $\Delta t_{\mathrm{dump}}$.  A consumer SDR therefore
produces zero fringe amplitude within the very first integration dump, regardless
of the signal-to-noise ratio.

\subsection{Implication for the Hardware Comparison}

The $23.8\times$ SNR gap of Section~\ref{sec:real-comparison} attributed the
shortfall in measured solar excess entirely to the elevated effective system
temperature of the consumer SDR chain ($T_{\mathrm{sys,eff}}\approx
\SI{3\,600}{\kelvin}$).  The clock-offset analysis adds a second,
\emph{independent} reason why digital cross-correlation with unsynchronised
SDRs cannot recover a fringe: even if $T_{\mathrm{sys,eff}}$ were reduced to
the idealised \SI{150}{\kelvin} by adding a dedicated LNA, the time-averaged
fringe amplitude would remain zero because $\eta_{\mathrm{coh}}\approx0$ for
any consumer oscillator.

These two effects are therefore complementary barriers.  The minimum hardware
specification for digital solar interferometry at \SI{610}{\mega\hertz} with
\SI{43.2}{\second} dumps requires addressing \emph{both} simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.8em]
  \item a low-noise amplifier with noise figure $\lesssim\SI{1}{\decibel}$
        ($T_{\mathrm{rx}}\approx\SI{75}{\kelvin}$), reducing
        $T_{\mathrm{sys,eff}}$ from $\approx\SI{3\,600}{\kelvin}$ to
        $\approx\SI{300}{\kelvin}$; and
  \item a common clock reference, a GPSDO or a \SI{10}{\mega\hertz}
        distribution amplifier, injected into all digitiser clock inputs,
        bringing $\varepsilon$ below $10$~ppt and
        $\eta_{\mathrm{coh}}>0.9$.
\end{enumerate}
Meeting both conditions would increase the measured solar excess from the
hardware-measured $\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{meas}}=0.026$~dB to
$\approx0.026\times(3\,600/300)\times\eta_{\mathrm{coh}}
\approx\SI{0.31}{\decibel}$, consistent with the simulation prediction of
$\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sim}}=0.586$~dB.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Chapter Conclusion}

The simulation results reported in this chapter establish a multi-level validation of the
pipeline and place it in context against the open-source simulation landscape.

At the \emph{analytic level}, the simulated interferometer reproduces three independent
classical benchmarks: the $\sim$14-minute fringe period (spring equinox, $\delta=0^\circ$),
the $\jinc$-shaped visibility decay consistent with a resolved solar disk, and the dirty
image numerically identical to the sky convolved with the independently computed PSF.

At the \emph{sensitivity level}, the radiometer equation (Section~\ref{sec:snr-analysis})
confirms that the quiet Sun is detectable at SNR $\approx 1\,700$ per integration dump,
and that all classes of solar burst in Table~\ref{tab:detectability} are strongly detected.
The LSB-validation check (Eq.~\eqref{eq:lsb-check}) confirms correct dynamic-range
matching for hardware export.

At the \emph{fidelity level}, replacing the Gaussian primary beam with the real 4NEC2
Yagi pattern (Section~\ref{sec:beam-fidelity}) increases the predicted transit-averaged
power by $\approx25\,\%$ relative to the Gaussian approximation-a $\sim\!1~\si{dB}$
systematic that would appear as a calibration offset in any comparison with field measurements.

At the \emph{comparative level}, the structured comparison with \textsc{APSYNSIM}
(Section~\ref{sec:apsynsim}) demonstrates that the present pipeline goes beyond
educational visualisation to offer physics-based noise, real data ingestion, real antenna
beams, hardware export, and full reproducibility-capabilities that define the boundary
between an educational simulator and a measurement-grade design tool.

At the \emph{hardware diagnostic level}, Section~\ref{sec:real-comparison} presents the
first diagnostic comparison between the simulation and the Hoa~L\d{a}c analogue recording of
20~March~2025.  The solar excess above the noise floor is $\mathcal{E}_{\rm sim}=0.586~\si{dB}$
from the pipeline and $\mathcal{E}_{\rm meas}=0.026~\si{dB}$ from the hardware trace, a
factor of $23.8\times$ in linear SNR.  This discrepancy is quantitatively consistent with
the effective system temperature of the consumer SDR receiver chain
($T_{\rm sys,eff}\approx\SI{3\,600}{\kelvin}$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:tsys-backcalc}), which is
$24\times$ higher than the idealised model value.  This sanity check is not a full hardware
validation (which would require an end-to-end gain-calibrated measurement); it is a
\emph{diagnostic comparison} that corroborates the Friis signal-level model and
provides an in-situ estimate of the hardware system temperature at the level of
consistency expected from consumer-grade SDR equipment.

At the \emph{clock-synchronisation level}, Section~\ref{sec:clock-offset} showed that a
consumer crystal oscillator with $\varepsilon\approx1$~ppm produces a coherence time
$\Delta t_{\rm coh}\approx\SI{1.6}{\milli\second}$, four orders of magnitude shorter than
the \SI{43.2}{\second} dump, so digital cross-correlation with unsynchronised digitisers
yields zero fringe amplitude regardless of SNR.  Together with the $T_{\rm sys}$ gap of
Section~\ref{sec:real-comparison}, this analysis defines the two concurrent hardware
upgrades required for a working digital solar interferometer: a low-noise amplifier
($T_{\rm rx}\lesssim\SI{75}{\kelvin}$) and a phase-coherent clock reference
($\varepsilon<10$~ppt, achievable with a GPSDO or shared \SI{10}{\mega\hertz} reference).
\clearpage
